A||||13||RossT||"break in oil"??||||1||
Z||000000||RossT||07-28-2000||01:06 AM||||I just bought a new Dodge Caravan to sit next to my two 88 fiero gt's.  The dealer told me that the van has break in oil, and not to change it for 3000 miles. Is this just regular oil?? can I change it sooner?? I normally change my oil every 2000 miles.<P>I did mention the word "fiero" so this thread is fiero related.  Plus it sits next to my two fieros!!<P>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000001||lowCG||07-28-2000||01:33 AM||||It's pretty much just 30W non-detergent motor oil,some manufacturers have their own brand,so I can't say for sure what's in there.I might just change the filter,and add a quart,but I'd wait to do the change.<BR>I know that they used to use that oil for running in a new engine,but thought that manufacturers had gone back to using just regular oil.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LarryB||07-28-2000||06:31 AM||||3000 miles sounds awfully high though for the first oil in a new engine, I thought 1000 miles was more typical.  Is there a recommendation in the owners manual? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||07-28-2000||08:08 AM||||Last fall my parents bought a brand new Chrysler mini-van, and the owners manual, and dealer, both said not to change the oil until it hit 3,000 miles. <P>Well, it hit 12,000 miles the other week, and we are now using Mobil1 in it. I have heard not to use synthetic on a brand new engine, because it is possible the piston rings will not seat properly.<P>But since a Caravan is a Chrysler product, it coulde be "just a Chrysler thing".||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||07-28-2000||08:46 AM||||since you bought it new, just to make sure your warranty wont be affected, Id do just what they tell you.<P>when i do engine rebuilds, I use aircraft break in oil. Its mineral based, non detergent. It allows you max power from the start, you dont have to pamper it. I just let it run and rev it up n down for an hour or so, then its good to go. I only leave it in for 500 miles or so. They tell you to not drive hard or too fast with regular auto engine break-in, but you cant have that luxury in an aircraft, you HAVE to use max power settings from every takeoff and flight. This is just way I do it, everyone has their own way, but thought id just pass it on.<P>rg||||reg||1
Z||000005||Megafiero||07-28-2000||09:34 AM||||When if comes to changing oil, there is one saying that comes to my mind, You can never changed your oil to offten, only wait too long.  Most the time I would sugest changing oil in a new engine after the first 500 miles.<P>Megafiero||||reg||1
Z||000006||stsmithgt||07-28-2000||10:51 AM||||Sounds like a lot of old school going on here.  engines get THOUSANDS of more miles out of them these days so maybe break in times have to change too.  I agree with following what the dealer says to do.  I WAS used to rebuilding a motor when it was looking at 100,000 miles.  Today that seems like it's just getting going. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> AND NO HOT RODDING DURING THIS TIME! Go drive up a mountain or tow a boat around to put a load on the motor.(I'd put the boat in the water too.)he he.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||07-28-2000||11:26 AM||||The owners manual that came with my parents mini-van actually said to occasionally do full throttle accelerations from 0 up to 45-50mph. You never know who to believe these days. Since they are just leasing this mini-van and have no plans to buy it at the end of the lease, we did several full throttle accelerations during break-in like the manual said, but if they were buying it, we wouild have pampered it much more.||||reg||1
Z||000008||gixxer||07-28-2000||06:51 PM||||It's a no-brainer. Just do what the owners manual tells you to do. The manufacturer is the authority on what the engine's break-in requirements are. <BR>The oil probably has the equivalent of GM's "Engine Oil Supplement" in it, and that's why they want you to keep it in for awhile.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||07-28-2000||06:57 PM||||The reason being is mainly for the camshaft to be broken in properly. If you ever did a cam change the is an oil additive so the lobes don't get cooked and have premature wear.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierospeeder||07-29-2000||12:40 AM||||i can tell you whats the break procedure for the vw beetle engine.<BR>and the acceleration is suppose to break in the rings.<P>I know when you first start a brand new engine, you want to run it higher then 2000 rpms to break in the camshaft. for 10 minutes<BR>to lube the cam and lifters so a wear pattern couldn't start<P>"during breakin, the close-tolerance parts are going to machine themselves into perfect adjustment through their movement."<P>To seat the rings, you want to create equal pressure on both sides of the rings, accelerate 20-55mph at 3/4 throttle then close the throttle and coast.   at least 5 times.<P>25miles to completely seat the valves.  <P>vw says to change the oil at 300 miles, then at the standard intervals, the author prefers to change the oil at 24 miles then again 300.  Use any oil, SF grade oil you prefer, single or multigrade is fine, there is no need to use non detergent oil for break-in so why bother.<P>most engines are broken in at 300 miles, they may loosen up a little more over the next 2000.  Harder rings(chrome) may take 500=1000 miles before oil consumption drops, signaling ring seating.<P>this is from a vw engine rebuilding book, mostly the bug engines.  Ive read these tips on most cars and i seen break in procedures in the fiero also.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||webbee||07-29-2000||01:15 AM||||You should change the filter every time you change the oil. Even at the 300 mile mark. <P>OK, everyone knew that but I just had to post too. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||lowCG||07-29-2000||09:15 AM||||I always have done what fierospeeder has described,except over a longer period of time,to allow the engine to keep from overheating,do the under-load driving while the car isn't quite wared up,then stop for coffee,or lunch somewhere,and do it again on the way back. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||07-29-2000||12:14 PM||||According to Chrysler the oil is suposed to be left alone in ALL chrysler product for 3000 miles. All the car makers have similar rules now. Follow them. <P>No, never use synthetic for "break in." It's not just a rumor thing. It really can keep things from seating in right. A good rebuild will also take a couple thousand miles to really seat well. You can use a short oil change cycle for the first couple thou to wash out mashine debrise but use regular 10-30 oil and don't beat on it much untill at least 3-5000. (Most ring lifter and cam makers tell you this right in the instruction books.) Even "race only" engines are usually run in with regular oil before using a synthetic. ||||reg||1
